From 2ba6f6eb77e5b25a2db40a64d86be0edd36ff6d8 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Jeffrey Frederick <5095066+jeffreyfrederick@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Mon, 23 Nov 2026 21:20:52 -0600
Subject: [PATCH 4/6] Apply nested clips as separate shapes so rect/rrect clips
 stay analytic

`clipping_system` intersects each view's overflow clip with its parent's
clip using `SkPathOps`, so a rounded container nested in the window (or a
rect container nested in a rounded one) ends up with a generic path, e.g.
the window rect intersected with a 10 px-radius panel. Skia's GPU backend
cannot apply such a clip analytically and falls back to a software clip
mask: rasterized on the CPU and uploaded as a new texture for every draw,
every frame (the masks are tied to the frame's clip-stack state, so they
are never reused across frames). Every view inside a rounded panel paid
for this.

Keep the clip of each entity also as the list of shapes it is the
intersection of (`CachedData::clip_shapes`, ancestors first, shared via
`Rc` with children that add no clip of their own) and apply them one by
one with `clip_rect` / `clip_rrect` (falling back to `clip_path` for
other shapes such as smoothed corners). The combined `clip_path` is still
computed for `DrawContext::clip_path()`.

Tested on the ChipDAW GUI prototype (all content inside a 10 px-radius
panel), Windows/NVIDIA, 60 Hz: dragging a clip in a 200-clip
arrangement went from ~53% to ~17% process CPU; before, ~85% of that
was `ClipStack::GetSWMaskFP` -> texture upload, on a 1420x568 mask.
Pixel diff of a static frame: 9 px differ (anti-aliasing at a panel
corner), apart from the window border.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
